  "response": "Scholars adjudicate a contested attribution by moving from internal to external evidence: first the stylistic fingerprint of the text itself, then the physical and documentary traces that tie a particular hand to a particular artifact. The clearest modern test case is a novel of American political life. Primary Colors: A Novel of Politics was published in 1996 by Random House. The author of Primary Colors was credited only as \"Anonymous\" on the title page. Donald Foster was a professor of English at Vassar College. Because the title page withheld a name, the hunt for one became irresistible, and the novel's publication was followed in short order by the public identification that this concealment provoked. Foster identified Joe Klein as the author of Primary Colors in New York magazine in February 1996, and a naming that early cannot be squared with the story that Klein confessed to writing the novel in a 1998 Newsweek column.\n\nThe reasoning behind that identification is the ordinary machinery of stylometry. Foster's attribution rested on recurrent words and phrases shared with Klein's signed journalism; that is, the published text contained distinctive lexical habits found in Klein's signed journalism. Internal evidence of this sort rarely stands alone in a well-documented case, and here at least one of two traces lies in the record, perhaps both: the distinctive lexical habits of the published book, and annotations carried on the manuscript in Joe Klein's own handwriting.\n\nWhat ultimately settles such a dispute, however, is less a matter of probability than of the document. The Washington Post retained document examiner Maureen Casey Owens to compare the manuscript annotations with samples of Klein's handwriting. The commission was issued first, and only afterward did the examiner reach a verdict. The document examiner retained by the Washington Post determined that the handwriting on the manuscript was Joe Klein's, and that determination shows that Joe Klein wrote Primary Colors. That verdict is also what shows the annotations borne by the manuscript to be the work of Klein's own hand, and it is corroboration of this physical kind, rather than any tally of favored words, that brings a quarrel over authorship to an end.\n\nThe limits of a purely statistical argument are visible in the classic precedent of the Federalist Papers. Frederick Mosteller and David Wallace published a statistical study of the disputed Federalist Papers in 1964. The 1964 statistical study attributed the twelve disputed papers to Alexander Hamilton. Any ascription of those twelve papers to Alexander Hamilton cannot be reconciled with the finding that Mosteller and Wallace actually reached, for their study concluded that James Madison wrote all twelve of the disputed Federalist Papers. The general lesson for attribution studies is therefore twofold. Quantitative stylistics can narrow a field of candidates and can render one of them overwhelmingly probable, but the question is decided only when that computational case converges with external testimony that no rival candidate can produce: a manuscript in a known hand, a publisher's records, or an admission from the writer. Where such testimony is absent, as in most anonymous works of antiquity, the attribution remains a weighted judgment rather than a settled fact.",
